        {
            "number": 2,
            "revision": "5736876230b0e27d9d04597cb9b9e5562b11c1b7",
            "parents": [
                "6eef6ff9d8c7e7839ceaf2ce9ba8e2f041cf7cb3"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/06/90006/2",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "Fred Chen",
                "email": "Fred.Chen@quantatw.com",
                "username": "fredchenquanta"
            },
            "createdOn": 1778140272,
            "author": {
                "name": "Fred Chen",
                "email": "Fred.Chen@quantatw.com",
                "username": "fredchenquanta"
            },
            "kind": "NO_CODE_CHANGE",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "overall sounds like a useful feature \ud83d\udc4d"
                },
                {
                    "file": "schemas/legacy.json",
                    "line": 1419,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "This sounds like a very specific workaround.  What happens when someone needs to ignore 3 or 4?  Should we instead add a property of how many errors to ignore before reporting?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "schemas/legacy.json",
                    "line": 1419,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "nitpick:\n\n> Sensors behind I3C hubs occasionally report single spurious readings, such as impossible RPM spikes or temperatures dropping to sub-zero, which quickly revert to the normal range.\n\nThis applies to all thresholds for a specific sensor channel.\n\nSo we should probably not configure it per-threshold since it will always apply to all of the thresholds on a given channel.\n\ne.g. `temp1_input` will have this on all it's thresholds or none of them?\n\nAlternatively one could accept the config bloat and do some kind of sanity check on it to prevent it being mis-configured, but i did not see anything of that sort."
                },
                {
                    "file": "schemas/legacy.json",
                    "line": 1419,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jagpal S Gill",
                        "email": "paligill@gmail.com",
                        "username": "jagpalgill"
                    },
                    "message": "+1, its better to define this as error tolerance count."
                },
                {
                    "file": "schemas/legacy.json",
                    "line": 1419,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Fred Chen",
                        "email": "Fred.Chen@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "fredchenquanta"
                    },
                    "message": "I originally made this configurable per-threshold because different threshold levels may need different tolerance policies. For example, users may want to tolerate transient low/negative temperature readings, but still log upper critical temperature conditions as early as possible.\n\nFor misconfiguration prevention, would it make sense to enforce that within the same direction, a more severe level's ToleranceCount does not exceed that of a less severe one?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "schemas/legacy.json",
                    "line": 1419,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Fred Chen",
                        "email": "Fred.Chen@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "fredchenquanta"
                    },
                    "message": "Done"
                },
                {
                    "file": "schemas/legacy.json",
                    "line": 1419,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "Assume the sensor returns \n\ncase 1: 3000 rpm, then 9999 rpm (wrong, cross warning threshold), then 3100 rpm.\ncase 2: 3000 rpm, then 99999 rpm (wrong, cross critical threshold), then 3100 rpm.\n\nDo we then assert the critical threshold in case 2 but do not assert the warning threshold in case 1?\n\nIf the reading is wrong, the value of that 'wrong' should not influence system behavior. Because otherwise system behavior would get unpredictable and depend on that random value.\n\n\nIf you want to see a sensor crossing the critical threshold as early as possible, and meanwhile have sensors occasionally reading a wrong value,\ni would suggest that on crossing the threshold, the sensor daemon should immediately read the sensor again, (based on the number of `ToleranceCount`) to then confirm if the threshold has to be asserted.\n\nThat is based on the assumption that there will only be `ToleranceCount` wrong readings in sequence, independent of the time between readings.\n\nIf you are instead talking about reading spikes in power-on / power-off phase then quickly repeated readings will not fix that and instead a time delay is needed between power state event and (re)starting sensor readings."
                },
                {
                    "file": "schemas/legacy.json",
                    "line": 1419,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Fred Chen",
                        "email": "Fred.Chen@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "fredchenquanta"
                    },
                    "message": "I agree that wrong readings should be filtered out. Using the same retry/filtering behavior across all thresholds in the same direction might be a cleaner approach. If a massive spike occurs (like Case 2), it could be confusing if only the Critical threshold gets asserted or only the Warning threshold logs a SEL.\n\nTo achieve an immediate re-read in dbus-sensors, we would need to dynamically set the waitTimer of restartRead to 0 ms for the next round.\nSince both transient noise and time-delay scenarios could happen, which implementation do you prefer?\n\nOption 1 (Interval-based Retry): Keep the current polling rate (e.g., 0.5s/1s) for retries. The time delay is implicitly toleranceCount \u00d7 pollingRate, so users can tune the delay by adjusting the count.\n\nOption 2 (Immediate Retry): Re-read immediately by default. If a time delay is needed, we might introduce a new parameter to configure the retry interval."
                },
                {
                    "file": "schemas/legacy.json",
                    "line": 1419,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "> Option 1 (Interval-based Retry): Keep the current polling rate (e.g., \n0.5s/1s) for retries. The time delay is implicitly toleranceCount \u00d7 \npollingRate, so users can tune the delay by adjusting the count.\n\nIf you were to implement it like that without also configuring your polling rate, dbus-sensors maintainers would not be able to change the default polling interval without possibly breaking your config.\n\nOption 2 sounds good. Re-read immediately to filter out wrong readings and then just have a `ToleranceDuration` config property to filter out time delay scenarios."
                },
                {
                    "file": "schemas/legacy.json",
                    "line": 1422,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "Could you explain why you added `NotRequired` if you are not using it in any config.\n\nWhen would someone configure this value.\n\nIf you intended to avoid introducing new optional properties to the threshold configuration interface (more in line with D-Bus spec), all the existing instances would have to be adjusted as well."
                },
                {
                    "file": "schemas/legacy.json",
                    "line": 1422,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Fred Chen",
                        "email": "Fred.Chen@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "fredchenquanta"
                    },
                    "message": "I initially followed the PEC pattern as a placeholder, but since the feature is disabled by default, NotRequired wouldn\u2019t be used in the scenario. \nI\u2019ve switched it to a numeric ToleranceCount."
                },
                {
                    "file": "schemas/legacy.json",
                    "line": 1422,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "Thanks for explaining it \ud83d\udc4d"
                }
            ],
