            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "schemas/legacy.json",
                    "line": 1422,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "Should we have a maximum value of `ToleranceCount`?\n\nI agree that it would have to be an arbitrary value but i don't want to see someone to configure a tolerance for 23839 wrong readings in a row.\n\nAt some maximum delay / reading count the sensor should assert so this feature should probably be bounded."
                },
                {
                    "file": "schemas/legacy.json",
                    "line": 1422,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "> I agree that it would have to be an arbitrary value but i don't want to see someone to configure a tolerance for 23839 wrong readings in a row.\n\nIf that is what they think is best, why not?\n\nIf we pick 16 someone will say that isn't good enough... what value makes people happy?  I think it is best to not be a nanny.\n\n(I've had to deal with a very similar thing here where the post code daemon decided to put a maximum number of post codes at something like 255 and it took months to get a higher maximum value allowed)."
                },
                {
                    "file": "schemas/legacy.json",
                    "line": 1422,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "> If we pick 16 someone will say that isn't good enough...\n\n16 would work well for me.\n\n> (I've had to deal with a very similar thing here where the post code \ndaemon decided to put a maximum number of post codes at something like \n255 and it took months to get a higher maximum value allowed).\n\nThat's a people problem you are describing though, not a technical problem with the limit itself.\n\nIn case someone needs to ignore a higher number of garbage readings they simply adjust the threshold.\n\nOtherwise it would be possible to configure a completely dead sensor which has lots of thresholds but cannot assert one in a reasonable time-frame.\n\nI'm somewhat on the side of preventing misconfiguration because misconfiguration has caused my own contributions to be delayed and having loose schemas like that demands more effort from the reviewer."
                },
                {
                    "file": "schemas/legacy.json",
                    "line": 1422,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Is 23839 a technically wrong value or just a likely not intended value?  If it is a wrong value, sure give me a limit above which everything else is a wrong value.  If it is a \"likely not intended value\" it isn't known if it is a misconfiguration or not.  You're being a nanny.\n\n> In case someone needs to ignore a higher number of garbage readings they simply adjust the threshold.\n\nI don't know how this is any different than the example I gave you.  It was a \"simple matter of updating a meson.options\".  Except it took months to get a change approved.  It doesn't matter if it is a \"technical problem\" or a \"people problem\" to me.  Why put ourselves through it?\n\n> Otherwise it would be possible to configure a completely dead sensor which has lots of thresholds but cannot assert one in a reasonable time-frame.\n\nIf we can come up with some \"reasonable [maximum] time-frame\"... ok.  Let's express it in terms of that then."
                },
                {
                    "file": "schemas/legacy.json",
                    "line": 1422,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "> If we can come up with some \"reasonable [maximum] time-frame\"... ok.  Let's express it in terms of that then.\n\nIMO that's a completely different thing. Either a sensor may produce a number of wrong readings in sequence (independent of the time between readings)\n\nor maybe the circuit it is measuring glitches for a bounded duration (perhaps up to a maximum deviation, not crossing a critical threshold?)\n\nIMO these are completely different things and need different solutions.\n\nI don't know which one it is and there was no time series of the actual correct and measured (wrong) values provided with the patch. \n\nI don't want to ask for that data, but would like to mention that solving it incorrectly will break things if the time between readings changes.\n\nAnyways i thought the maximum value would be a value add (ability to reason about all upstream configs) but maybe it's not, resolving this."
                }
            ],
